Dragons are *slow*.  Stay out of melee
range and kill them with ranged attacks.

> For armed monks, would a shield be in order?

The wiki suggests a small shield.  It prevents
you from dealing a staggering blow, but if
you're using a weapon for melee that's not
really an issue.

> Also, what types of armor are used for monks?

Anything you could wear at the same time as
mail is as good for monks as for any other role.
Anything that goes in the mail slot, however,
will make it basically impossible to hit anything.

> The castle had GoP,

Wear those until you get gauntlets of dexterity
(and good strength, but if you've made it to
the castle you probably have that), then trade
if you have enough enchant scrolls to swing
it without an AC penalty.

> BoS(already had them),

Yes, you want boots, and speed boots are
an excellent choice.

> invis cloak(already invis), and cloak of displacement.

Either of those provides MC3, which you want,
but if you still have your robe that also has MC3.

Displacement may also cause monsters to
miss you, which can be nice, but it's not reliable,
and the spellcasting benefit from your robe is
probably better for a Monk if you have any
good combat spells, particularly magic missile
or charm monster.  (For non-combat spells, like
identify and remove curse, you can switch out
for your robe just to cast the spell then switch
back to the other cloak after, and anyway the
failure rate is less of a problem if you don't
have the time pressures of combat.)

If you find a cloak of magic resistance, you can
decide whether you want to trade up to that
(allowing you to trade up to a higher-damage
weapon than Magicbane) or keep the robe (for
better spellcasting).  Wishing for the Eye or
some other MR-artifact would make this moot,
but personally I usually only do that if I've not
seen enough artifacts yet to introduce a chance
of wish failure.  I don't trust the RNG.


